WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.160
- [Dion] Many crypto enthusiasts rejoiced

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.500
when the Securities and Exchange
Commission gave companies

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.410
a green light to sell exchange
traded funds in the US

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.850
that directly hold Bitcoin.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.290
In just a few days of trading,

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.450
it became pretty clear
who the big winners were.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.790
I'm going to tell you
who's winning so far,

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.130
why they're winning,
and what this could mean

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.320
for the future of crypto investment.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.220
Let's get into it.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.210
BlackRock, which boast
more than $10 trillion

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:25.620
of assets under management,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.020
was the big winner on the
first few days of trading.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.320
BlackRock's iShares Bitcoin Trust ETF,

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.460
known by the ticker IBIT,
brought in almost $500 million

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.980
and had more than $1.6 billion

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.080
of trading volume in the first two days.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.490
According to Bloomberg Data,

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:45.240
Fidelity's Bitcoin ETF had
about $400 million in inflows

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.150
and did about $1.1 billion in volume.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.790
The fund from Kathy Woods'
ARC Investment Management

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.640
had around $111 million of inflows

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.090
and just under $450
million of trading volume.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.220
Other funds brought in just a fraction

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:00.330
of BlackRock's haul.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.240
ETFs from companies like
Valkyrie and WisdomTree

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.310
each saw around $11 million

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.470
of volume in their first
two days of trading,

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.570
as my colleague Jack Pitcher reported.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.090
Analysts predict that the
firm that draws the most funds

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:16.200
early will have an advantage
to become the go-to Bitcoin ETF

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:17.880
for institutional investors.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.460
Since there were nearly a
dozen ETFs made available

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.130
to the public the day
after the SEC's decision,

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.800
no company could get an
advantage by being first,

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.580
so asset managers almost
universally cut their fees.

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:32.730
BlackRock originally planned
a management fee of 0.3%

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.940
for its ETF, but after it was
revealed that other managers

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:40.050
were charging lower fees,
they cut their fee to 0.25%,

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.260
and they lowered it to
0.12% for the first year

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.633
or until the fund holds
more than $5 billion.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.860
In addition to the lower
fee, BlackRock's IBIT holds

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.380
around $500 million worth of Bitcoin.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.260
That's more Bitcoin
than any other spot ETF,

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:58.383
with the exception of the
Grayscale Bitcoin Trust ETF.

00:01:59.507 --> 00:02:01.350
Investors favor funds
that hold more assets

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.080
because it makes it easier
to buy and sell the fund.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:06.720
That's especially important
with assets like crypto

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.060
that can have big swings in price.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:11.813
Grayscale's ETF actually
holds 50 times more Bitcoin

00:02:11.813 --> 00:02:14.580
than BlackRock's and has
seen more than double

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:17.070
the trading volume, but over
the first two days of trading,

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.750
those flows were all negative

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.510
and investors pulled
almost $600 million out

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:22.950
according to Bloomberg's data.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:26.010
One big factor, Grayscale's
ETF charges a fee

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.320
that's six times higher than BlackRock's

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.990
and most other spot Bitcoin ETFs.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:34.260
The numbers so far suggest
that investors dislike fees

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.573
more than they like large asset holdings.

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.350
So what does this mean for the
future of crypto investing?

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:42.960
Having BlackRock as
the primary Bitcoin ETF

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:44.550
could make a big difference.

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:47.430
First, it could mean a
lot more Bitcoin buying.

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:49.830
ETF asset managers need to buy and hold

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:52.140
sufficient quantities
of the underlying asset,

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.020
so as more people buy their Bitcoin ETF,

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.270
BlackRock will buy more Bitcoin,

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:00.270
and with $10 trillion of
assets under management,

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:02.370
BlackRock has a lot of buying power.

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:05.460
BlackRock and others have
already started making a pitch

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.800
to more traditional investors
about why they should

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:08.850
get into crypto.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.280
Bringing in traditional
institutional investors

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.070
who manage millions or
even billions of dollars

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.200
is a big part of why the fees are so low.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.990
To be profitable with fees below 0.3%,

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.360
funds will need to
bring in a lot of money,

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.930
into the tens of billions of
dollars, says Eric Balchunas,

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:27.600
the Senior ETF analyst for
Bloomberg's research arm.

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.660
The most popular Bitcoin
ETF also stands to bring in

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.110
a new group of investors who
haven't had a lot of exposure

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:35.580
to traditional investing.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.550
That could be very profitable
as those investors get older

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:40.489
and invest more money in the future.

00:03:40.489 --> 00:03:42.900
Bitcoin ETFs could also
create a dividing line

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.190
between providers.

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.040
Vanguard, like BlackRock,
is known for its low cost

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.290
investment vehicles, but Vanguard has said

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:52.740
it won't offer access to the
Bitcoin ETF on its platform.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:55.680
The big picture is that based
on the early days of trading,

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.870
BlackRock is looking
well positioned to win

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.990
a lot of benefits from the
newly approved Bitcoin ETF.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:03.120
That could set them up to dominate Bitcoin

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.180
and new digital assets that
are approved by the SEC

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.765
for years to come.

00:04:07.765 --> 00:04:10.515
(cheerful music)

